    "id": "train-5275",
    "question": "Bert, Ernie, and Peggy collect stamps.  Bert has four times as many stamps as Ernie, but Ernie has three times as many stamps as Peggy.  If Peggy currently has 75 stamps in her collection, how many stamps does she need to add to her collection to have a collection as large as Bert's collection?",
    "gold": "825",
    "answer": "Ernie has three times as many stamps as Peggy, or 3*75=<<3*75=225>>225 stamps.\nBert has 4 times as many stamps as Ernie, or 4*225=<<4*225=900>>900 stamps.\nThus, Peggy would need 900-75=<<900-75=825>>825 more stamps to have a collection as large as Bert's.\n#### 825"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many stamps Peggy needs to add to her collection to have as many stamps as Bert, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the number of stamps Ernie has:**\n   Ernie has three times as many stamps as Peggy. Since Peggy has 75 stamps, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Ernie's stamps} = 3 \\times 75 = 225\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the number of stamps Bert has:**\n   Bert has four times as many stamps as Ernie. Since Ernie has 225 stamps, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Bert's stamps} = 4 \\times 225 = 900\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the number of stamps Peggy needs to add:**\n   To have a collection as large as Bert's, Peggy needs to add the difference between Bert's and Peggy's current number of stamps. Since Peggy currently has 75 stamps, we calculate:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Stamps Peggy needs to add} = 900 - 75 = 825\n   \\]\n\nThus, the number of stamps Peggy needs to add to her collection to have as many stamps as Bert is \\(\\boxed{825}\\).",
